1. What do all the methods (move(), special_move(),  use()) in the previous activities have in common?
They all share the same method name across different classes and serve a similar purpose tailored to each specific class.

2. How does this activity show polymorphism in a gaming context?
This shows polymorphism by showing how a single function can process completely different types of objects through one interface. 

3. Can you think of other examples where the same action behaves differently depending on the character or item?

The attack() Action
    Archer: Fires a long-range projectile arrow.
    Knight: Swings a close-range physical sword.
    Necromancer: Casts a life-stealing spell.
The take_damage() Action
    Standard Enemy: Loses health points directly.
    Armored Boss: Reduces incoming damage by 50% before losing health.
    Ghost Enemy: Evades physical damage entirely but takes magic damage.
The interact() Action
    Chest: Triggers an opening animation and drops loot.
    NPC: Opens a dialogue box or shop menu.
    Lever: Toggles a mechanical door or bridge.
